-- title:
--   The abelian-quotient class map on finite-part m-torsion
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N_0 \neq 0$ and a prime $p$ with $p \nmid N_0$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}$ satisfying `A.LiesOverPrime p`, i.e. $p$ lies in the non-units of $A$, a level datum $\Lambda$ of type `LevelData N₀ p A` — in particular a structure morphism $\sigma_A \colon \operatorname{Spec} A \to$ `base p` with $\mathrm{barPt}\,A$ followed by $\sigma_A$ equal to `genPt p`, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with morphism $\Lambda.f$ carrying a relative group law, and bijections $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$, $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ of `JZero N₀`, resp. `JZeroC (ResidueField ↥A) N₀`, with the sections of $\Lambda.f$ over `genPt p`, resp. over $\mathrm{resPt}\,A$ followed by $\sigma_A$ — together with the hypothesis $h\Lambda$ that $\Lambda$ `IsJacobian` (abelian-scheme property bundle for $\Lambda.f$, commutativity of the group law, additivity of $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ and of $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$, Galois equivariance of $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$, compatibility of reduction mod $\ell$, and a Hecke action; summarised here), an object $O$ of type `JZeroNeronObjectAtP N₀ p hpN₀ A hA Λ`, an index $i \in \{0,1\}$ and a natural number $m$. Then there exists an additive group homomorphism $\nu$ from the subgroup $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,m$ of `JZero (N₀ * p)` — generated by those $x$ lying in `jZeroTorsion (N₀ * p) m` and satisfying `ExtendsToPlace A Λ.σA (O.pts x)` — to `JZeroC (ResidueField ↥A) N₀` such that, first, for every $x$ in that subgroup and every $s \colon \operatorname{Spec} A \to O.G$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $(O.\mathrm{pts}\,x).1$ equal to $\mathrm{barPt}\,A$ followed by $s$, one has $\nu(x) = \Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}\bigl(\mathrm{fibreMap}\,(O.\mathrm{abqFibre}\,i)\,\bar s\bigr)$, where $\bar s$ denotes $\mathrm{resPt}\,A$ followed by $s$ regarded as a section over $\mathrm{resPt}\,A$ followed by $\sigma_A$ and `O.abqFibre i` is the corresponding morphism between the base changes of $O.g$ and $\Lambda.f$; and, second, $m \cdot \nu(x) = 0$ for all $x$.
--
--   This provides the specialisation, or abelian-quotient, homomorphism $\nu_i$ on the finite part of the $m$-torsion of $J_0(N_0p)$ at a place above $p$: the prescription via an $A$-valued extension $s$ of a given $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}$-point is independent of the choice of $s$ (uniqueness of such extensions coming from separatedness, via [`AlgebraicGeometry.SchemeHomOver.ext_of_forall_algebraicClosure_point_of_isReduced_of_flat`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.SchemeHomOver.ext_of_forall_algebraicClosure_point_of_isReduced_of_flat)) and is additive with values killed by $m$. It is the input to [`ModularCurve.JZeroNeronObjectAtP.hasLowerLevelTorsion_of_ptsSp_symm_fibreMap_abqFibre_ne_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZeroNeronObjectAtP.hasLowerLevelTorsion_of_ptsSp_symm_fibreMap_abqFibre_ne_zero) and to [`ModularCurve.JZeroNeronObjectAtP.heckeTorsion_ne_bot_of_ptsSp_symm_fibreMap_abqFibre_ne_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZeroNeronObjectAtP.heckeTorsion_ne_bot_of_ptsSp_symm_fibreMap_abqFibre_ne_zero), in the level-lowering analysis of the reduction of $J_0(N_0p)$ at $p$.
